# What must not reach the build context, and why each entry is here.
#
# This file is `machinery` under AD-2, exactly as the `Dockerfile` beside it is:
# materialized components ship no Dockerfile (AD-15) and therefore ship no
# ignore file either. It is listed explicitly as `machinery` when Epic 7 authors
# `accelerator.toml`, because AD-2's input reconciliation fails a path claimed by
# no disposition.
#
# Two failures are being prevented, and they are not the same failure. The first
# is bloat: `dist/`, `site/` and the tool caches are build outputs of the *host*
# and would be copied into the image to be used by nothing. The second is
# smuggling: `.env`, `.envs/`, `db.sqlite3` and `.local-dev-keys/` are local
# state, and an image carrying any of them is a payload configured by a file
# rather than by the environment -- which is exactly what FR-38 forbids and what
# `tests/unit/test_payload_properties.py` asserts against.
#
# What deliberately stays in the context: `pixi.toml`, `pixi.lock`,
# `pyproject.toml`, `manage.py` and `src/`, which are what the image is; plus
# `component.toml`, `README.md` and `LICENSE`, which the editable self-install
# needs -- `pyproject.toml` names the README and the licence file, and
# `component.toml` is source that travels rather than configuration.

# Version control. Excluded deliberately, and it has a consequence the Dockerfile
# handles rather than works around: hatch-vcs derives `dynamic = ["version"]`
# from git tags, so the build supplies the version explicitly instead of
# shipping the history.
.git/
.gitattributes
.gitignore

# The host's own pixi environment. Several hundred megabytes of osx-arm64
# packages that the image would never load; `pixi install --locked` builds the
# linux-64 environment inside the image from `pixi.lock`.
.pixi/

# Build and documentation outputs of the host.
dist/
site/
staticfiles/

# Local state. `db.sqlite3` is a real local database sitting at the repository
# root; shipping it would put a writable credential store into the payload.
db.sqlite3
db.sqlite3-journal
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml

# Configuration, which a payload does not carry (FR-38). All are gitignored and
# none may ever enter an image.
#
# **`**/` is load-bearing here, and this is the difference from `.gitignore`.**
# A `.dockerignore` pattern is matched against the path *relative to the build
# context root*, so a bare `.env` excludes exactly `./.env` and nothing else --
# `src/config/.env` would sail past it and into the image through `COPY src
# ./src`. `.gitignore` matches a bare pattern at every level and is the habit
# this file has to be read against. Both spellings are kept: the root-anchored
# one because it is the file that actually exists, and the `**/` one because it
# is the one that makes the claim in this file's header true.
.env
**/.env
.env.*
**/.env.*
.envs/
**/.envs/

# The generated local development signing keypair. Gitignored, never committed,
# and a private key in an image is a private key in a registry.
.local-dev-keys/
**/.local-dev-keys/

# Tool caches. `__pycache__/` and `*.py[cod]` are `**/`-prefixed for the reason
# the configuration block above records, and it is not hypothetical here: ten
# `__pycache__` directories exist under `src/` in a working tree that has run the
# suite, and a root-anchored pattern excludes none of them -- they would ship
# into the image through `COPY src ./src`, which is the "excludes tool caches"
# claim in this file's header being false.
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
**/*.py[cod]

# Planning artefacts and agent machinery. None of it is the application.
_bmad/
_bmad-output/
.agents/
.bmad-loop/
.claude/
.github/

# The harness's own tests. They are `machinery` too -- see the module docstring
# of `tests/unit/test_payload_properties.py` -- and the image is the subject of
# those tests rather than their host.
tests/

# Host filesystem residue, at every level for the same reason as above.
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
